# Developer and Git cleaner

`cleaner dev` inventories regenerable project outputs and user-level language
caches. `cleaner git` keeps the existing guarded repository maintenance and
history-rewrite workflow.

## Developer clutter

The default read-only scan starts at the working directory. Set `--root` to a
project parent such as `~/Coding`; choose a home directory only when a full
user scan is worth the extra time. Results include logical size and entry count.

Project cleanup recognizes outputs only beside matching manifests. Coverage
includes Node.js dependencies and framework caches, Python tool caches and
reconstructable virtual environments, Rust and Maven `target`, Gradle and CMake
`build`, Swift `.build`, .NET `bin`/`obj`, Dart, Elixir, Haskell, Zig, and
Terraform outputs, Composer `vendor`, and CocoaPods `Pods`. A directory with a
valid `CACHEDIR.TAG` signature is also shown as explicitly regenerable cache
data.

Unless `--project-only` is selected, the inventory also asks installed Go, npm,
pnpm, pip, and uv tools for their active cache locations. Apply uses each
tool's native cleanup command. It additionally recognizes the user-owned Cargo,
Gradle, Maven, and NuGet cache locations without deleting tool configuration,
installed binaries, or source projects.

> **Review every path:** A cache tag is a strong hint, not authority by itself.
> The scanner never follows symlinks, crosses a filesystem boundary, deletes a
> directory based only on a generic name, or scans hidden application state when
> the selected root is the home directory. Close package managers, compilers,
> editors, and builds before applying.

```sh
cleaner dev
cleaner dev --root ~/Coding
cleaner dev --root ~/Coding --project-only
cleaner dev --root ~/Coding --apply
```

## Git repositories

`cleaner git` replaces error-prone `filter-branch` recipes with
`git-filter-repo`, keeps normal garbage collection conservative, and exposes
unreachable objects before removing them.

> **Rewrite warning:** History rewrites change commit IDs. Keep the generated
> bundle outside any later cleanup, coordinate force-pushes, and retain it
> until every required ref has been verified.

The `tool` is `git`; choose an operation, optionally point `--repo` at
another work tree, and use `--backup` to override the rewrite bundle path.

## Operations

### `audit`

Runs `git count-objects -vH` and a full `git fsck --unreachable --no-reflogs`.
It prints object metadata only, not object contents.

### `gc`

Expires unreachable reflog entries and runs `git gc`. The default
`--expire 30.days.ago` retains recent recovery points. Accepted values are
`N.day(s).ago`, `N.week(s).ago`, `N.month(s).ago`, `N.year(s).ago`, or `now`.
`now` and zero-duration values are rejected unless `--force` is also selected.

### `dangling`

Shows every unreachable object and a dry-run reflog expiry before applying.
Immediate and zero-duration expiry is rejected unless `--force` is selected.

### `rewrite`

Requires `git-filter-repo` and either `--path` or `--replace-text`. A
`--path` is removed from every commit by default; select `--keep-path` to keep
only that path. Before rewriting, the command creates a bundle containing every
ref. The working tree must be clean, even with `--force`; uncommitted and
untracked files are not stored in the bundle. Rewrites change commit IDs and can
remove the `origin` remote.

## Direct CLI

```sh
cleaner git audit
cleaner git gc --expire 90.days.ago
cleaner git dangling --expire 30.days.ago
cleaner git rewrite --path path/to/secret.env
cleaner git rewrite --replace-text replacements.txt
```

Nothing mutates without `--apply`. Direct applies require typing `APPLY`; the
shared TUI confirms with `CLEAN` and then supplies `--apply --yes`. Developer
apply repeats discovery, so the preview and final target set can differ if files
change between runs.

## Exit status

`0` means the inventory or requested maintenance completed. `1` means a
developer scan was incomplete or a cleanup command failed. `2` means the root,
repository, arguments, dependency, backup, or Git operation was unsafe or
failed. Invalid command-line syntax also exits `2`.
